[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Composition vs Inheritance and Liskov Substitution Pitfalls in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object-oriented programming in Java relies heavily on two core mechanisms for code reuse: inheritance and composition. While inheritance allows a class to acquire the properties and methods of another, it often introduces rigid coupling. Composition, on the other hand, builds complex types by combining objects that implement specific behaviors. Understanding when to use each, and mastering the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP), is essential for writing maintainable enterprise Java applications."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"The Inheritance Trap and LSP Violations"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Liskov Substitution Principle states that objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of its subclasses without breaking the application logic. A classic violation of LSP is the Rectangle-Square problem. Mathematically, a square is a rectangle. Programmatically, forcing a Square to inherit from a Rectangle leads to unexpected behavior."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"public class Rectangle {
    protected int width;
    protected int height;

    public void setWidth(int width) { this.width = width; }
    public void setHeight(int height) { this.height = height; }
    public int getArea() { return width * height; }
}

public class Square extends Rectangle {
    @Override
    public void setWidth(int width) {
        super.setWidth(width);
        super.setHeight(width);
    }

    @Override
    public void setHeight(int height) {
        super.setHeight(height);
        super.setWidth(height);
    }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"If a method expects a Rectangle and modifies its width and height independently, passing a Square will violate the caller's assumptions, causing silent bugs."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Fixing the Design with Composition"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Instead of forcing an inheritance hierarchy that does not fit reality, we can use interfaces and composition. By defining a Shape interface, both Rectangle and Square can implement their own area calculations without forcing artificial constraints on setters."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public interface Shape {
    int getArea();
}

public class Rectangle implements Shape {
    private final int width;
    private final int height;

    public Rectangle(int width, int height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    public int getArea() {
        return width * height;
    }
}

public class Square implements Shape {
    private final int side;

    public Square(int side) {
        this.side = side;
    }

    public int getArea() {
        return side * side;
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Takeaways"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer composition over inheritance to achieve loose coupling and avoid fragile base class problems."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure subclasses honor behavioral contracts, not just structural similarities, to satisfy the Liskov Substitution Principle."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use interfaces to define common behaviors rather than sharing state through deep inheritance hierarchies."}]
